WEBVTT

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.320
PIERO MORANO: Hello, everyone,

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.840
and welcome to our
Microsoft Build session

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.120
on the AI-Native Data Engineer.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.220
This is obviously a very
hot topic right now,

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.190
and I'm really excited to spend
this session exploring how AI is

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.770
changing the way
that engineers work,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.630
not just by generating code

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.050
but by helping us rethink the
full data developer workflow

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.200
across Fabric, VS Code, and CLI.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.050
My name is Piero Morano, and
I lead product management

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.270
for the Developer Experience
for Data Engineering

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.760
and Data Science in
Microsoft Fabric.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.810
In this session, we'll look

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.450
at how the data engineering
role is evolving,

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:40.650
how to choose the right power
tool for the right job,

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.280
and how Fabric can help data
engineers move faster while

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.210
staying in control

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.400
of architecture
quality and outcomes.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.740
I'm really excited to be
joined by Rocky Rackman,

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.830
who will bring a practical
data engineering perspective

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.190
and show how these ideas apply
in real Fabric scenarios.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.290
Raki, over to you
for a quick intro.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:58.620
RAKI RAHMAN: Hey.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:00:59.250
Thanks, Piero.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.000
Hi, everybody.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.230
My name is Raki Rahman.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.490
I'm a principal
software engineer

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.790
on the SQL Server Telemetry
and Intelligence team.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.670
We've been a Fabric customer for
about three or four years now.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.890
And I aspire to be an AI-native
data engineer, and I would love

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:14.450
to show some of the tips

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.070
and tricks we've picked
up along the way.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:16.980
Thank you, Piero.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:17.310
PIERO MORANO: Awesome.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.840
Thank you so much, Raki.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.270
Let me quickly walk
through the agenda today.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.670
So we'll start by
framing how the role

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.610
of the data engineer is
evolving in the age of AI.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.420
Then we'll look at two
important working styles,

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.570
the native IDE data engineer
who prefers VS Code,

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.490
a more developer-centric
workflow;

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.100
and briefly touch upon the
web-first data engineer

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.400
or analyst who works
directly in Fabric portal.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.970
From there, Raki will take us
into a practical Fabric scenario

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.420
and show how he builds
a pipeline in his work

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.350
of everyday work in
his team across using,

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.280
leveraging AI
across the process.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.160
For those of you new
to Microsoft Fabric,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.560
here's a quick foundation
before we dive deep

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.730
into the main topic.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:07.020
Microsoft Fabric brings together
the core experiences data teams

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.850
need in one unified platform.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.960
So, from databases to data
orchestration and analytics,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.420
real-time intelligence,
Power BI,

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.840
what makes it especially
important in the age of AI is

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.240
that these experiences
are not isolated.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.210
They are connected through the
Fabric platform with OneLake

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:28.980
as the common data foundation,
governance, and security.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.500
With that foundation in
mind, let's dive deeper

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.350
into how the role of data
engineer is evolving.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.600
Many of you are probably
already in the middle

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.760
of this transition
or revolution.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.280
You are experimenting with
AI tools in the browser,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.650
VS Code perhaps on the terminal,

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.870
and trying to understand
what this means

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.910
for your day-by-day work.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.740
On the left side
of these slides,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.260
we see the traditional
work of the data engineer:

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.830
building Pipelines, writing
ETL logic, debugging issues,

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:56.880
managing dependencies,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.780
making sure that the old
data flow works reliably.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.190
That work is still critical, but
the way we do it is changing.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:08.580
On the right-hand side, we
have the emerging AI-native

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:09.420
data engineer.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.370
This is not a different person.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.380
It is evolution of the same job.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.960
The engineer still owns the
architecture, the quality,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.810
and the end results but uses AI

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.120
to accelerate the
implementation.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:22.410
From a product standpoint,

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:26.640
this means investing not only
on-code generation but also

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.300
in the foundations that make
AI useful for real engineers.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.930
So more work on
API tools, skills,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.660
and better context
and experience.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.090
As you watch this session,
I encourage you to think

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:43.230
about which one of these tools
can help you be more productive

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.540
using AI.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.730
So, if the role of the
data engineer is changing,

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.640
the tools need to
evolve as well.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.610
The key point is that there
is no single best surface

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.710
for every data engineer
or every task.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:04:00.570
First, the Fabric web portal,
This is great for zero setup,

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.520
visual exploration
collaboration,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.350
and quick access to data.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.140
Second, IDE in VS Code.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.080
This is where many
professional developers

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.290
and data engineers are most
productive with GitHub Copilot,

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.180
source control, and more.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.430
Third, the CLI and
terminal experience.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.910
This is useful when the task is
more scriptable, repeatable,

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.340
and automation-focused.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.610
Not every task needs
the same AI experience.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.790
For exploration, debugging,
and ad hoc analysis,

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.070
you usually want a
conversational flow

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:36.270
where you stay close to the data
and guide the AI step by step.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.940
For more repeatable or
operational work, you may want

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.230
to delegate more

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:44.280
of the execution while
reviewing the plan and results.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.120
The key point is simple: Match
the AI interaction model

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.340
to the task, and keep
the engineer in control

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.920
of the important decision.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.660
So, before we jump
into the demo,

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.100
let me quickly clarify the
experiences I am referring to.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.190
First, we have the newly
enhanced Copilot Chat

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.100
in Fabric notebooks, which
helps user work directly

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.540
in the Fabric portal.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.390
Second, we have the Fabric
data engineering extension

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:12.360
in VS Code, now enhanced
with MCP and tools

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.480
that make the custom
GitHub Copilot agent aware

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.590
of Fabric context.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:21.720
Let me jump into the first demo

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:27.290
where I am reading an event
stream using a Spark notebook,

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:28.730
leveraging Kafka.

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:31.340
And this is bridging
the Spark and the RTI,

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.530
the real-time
intelligence in Fabric.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.380
The stream comes into
the notebook as a JSON,

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:37.820
and I would like to transform it

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.130
into a Delta table following
the best practices

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:43.820
of medallion architecture
and EventHub best practices.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:45.710
I'm not very familiar
with streaming,

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:49.100
so I decide to use Copilot
and see how it can help.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:50.190
Let's see what happened.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.830
UNIDENTIFIED PERSON 1: I am using
now Fabric Data Engineering new

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:56.340
VS Code extension, and I will
leverage the new Fabric-aware

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:59.160
notebook agent mode to integrate
a new social media feed

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.330
from event stream.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.820
I am reading the event
stream in my Spark notebook.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.620
The first cell is
a parameter cell,

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:07.320
and the second one connects
to the Fabric event stream

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.530
as a Kafka source, decoding
the binary key value columns

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.550
to strings.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:14.100
As I run the notebook cells
and events start arriving,

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.110
each record contains
a JSON payload

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.420
with customer feedback details.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.220
My goal is to ingest
this streaming

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.720
into our medallion architecture,

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.730
applying streaming
best practices.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:26.520
So, instead of manually
designing the ingestion pattern,

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.640
I ask Copilot, can you check
this notebook that read

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.730
from event streaming social
media feeds and suggest a plan

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.430
to incorporate it into our
medallion architecture

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.500
and eventhouse authoring
best practices

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.060
in my built-in folders?

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.120
Copilot interprets the notebook
and, after checking the skills,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.360
suggests a plan that starts
with Phase 1 Bronze Layer: One,

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.180
parse the value column, which
right now is just a big string

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.270
of JSON text; two,
add tracking columns,

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:53.880
suggesting to break it apart
into individual columns

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.820
as add metadata columns,
including ingestion timestamp

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.840
and batch ID so we can trace
every record back to its source;

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.170
three, write to a Delta bronze
table using append-only mode,

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.300
adding also a checkpoint
so, if the notebook crashes

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.760
and restarts, it picks up
exactly where it left off

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.630
to avoid duplicates or data
loss; four, validate; finally,

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:15.210
check row counts to make
sure data is flowing.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.460
But how did Copilot
figure all of this out?

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:18.810
It scanned the built-in folder

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.090
and discovered two
available skills:

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:24.630
E2E medallion architecture
and eventhouse authoring.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.610
By reading the medallion
architecture skill,

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:30.120
Copilot was able to generate
a solid implementation plan.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.460
Continuing with the silver
layer, the plan suggests reading

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.920
from the bronze Delta table
that we just created.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.690
From there, we apply
a few quality rules

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.840
like removing duplicates; then
do some schema conformance; and,

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.240
finally, curate data
to a silver table.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.100
The plan also suggests
additional steps,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:47.430
including a gold layer,
eventhouse optimizations,

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:50.670
and even a graphical view of
the proposed architecture.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.760
But I want to move in a more
controlled way, so I ask Copilot

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.760
to continue only with Phase
1 and Phase 2 for now,

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.420
meaning bronze and silver only.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:03.690
I also make the lakehouse
targets explicit: Retail raw LH

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:06.930
for bronze, retail
curated LH for silver.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:08.910
And I want to preserve
the setup I already have,

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:11.670
so I ask Copilot not to
modify the first two cells

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:14.940
in my notebook and, instead,
implement everything starting

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:18.300
from cell 3, keeping all the
new steps in the same notebook.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.820
Copilot identifies the
two lakehouses for bronze

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:25.080
and silver using Fabric notebook
MCP server and proceeds

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.110
with a plan maintaining the
first two cells untouched.

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:31.080
It also updates the notebook
with clear markdown cells,

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:32.580
very convenient
when collaborating

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.170
with other team members.

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:35.850
After a few iterations
with Copilot,

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:38.100
I now have the final
version of the notebook.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.920
It includes clear markdown
cells so the pipeline is easy

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.890
to understand and collaborate
on, and it also has a cell

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.960
to control the incoming
streaming data.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.110
At this point, I ask Copilot
to execute the notebook.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:51.690
I also ask it to skip
one debugging cell.

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:53.910
Copilot understands the
current cell organization

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:55.230
and runs the notebook.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:57.060
During execution, it
confirms the number

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:58.950
of incoming streaming
records being written

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:00.780
into the bronze
and silver tables.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:03.120
Once the run completes, I
can see the data flowing

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:04.440
through the pipeline,

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.620
transformed from the original
JSON events into Delta tables

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:09.630
in the bronze and
silver lakehouses.

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.030
This saved me a significant
amount of time.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.610
Before moving forward, I'll ask
another team member to review

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:16.410
and validate the notebook.

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:16.680
PIERO MORANO: Okay.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:20.100
Great. So now that we have
seen, we have a better idea

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:25.110
of what does it mean to build
a data pipeline using GitHub

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:28.110
Copilot on VS Code and
the VS Code extension,

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:29.340
the engineering extension.

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:34.070
I will hand it over to Raki to
have a better understanding

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:37.130
of how this can work in
real-life experience.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:38.270
Raki, over to you.

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:39.500
RAKI RAHMAN: Thank you, Piero.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:41.970
So we're going to
have some fun now.

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:49.440
The way I want to approach AI
is this is a TV show character.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:50.550
It's from The Simpsons.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:52.680
His name is Ralph Wiggum.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:54.300
So the way you can
think about AI,

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.430
or at least this is my
mindset of thinking about AI,

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:59.550
is Ralph Wiggum in The
Simpsons character,

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:03.390
he's a very charming,
very simple kid.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:06.600
He's not too bright, but
he's very hardworking.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:11.040
And the idea is that, when
you can give Ralph a task

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.610
and you can give him the hints
in order to go ahead and achieve

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:18.150
that task, he is the hardest
worker in the room in order

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:20.610
to achieve whatever
you've asked him to.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:23.850
And this concept in AI is
actually called a Ralph loop,

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:27.720
which you can take a look in
your favorite search engine.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:29.670
Go ahead and search up the
Ralph loop, and you'll notice

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:33.600
that this is picking up a lot
of momentum in the industry.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:39.210
And the idea is that you want to
basically go ahead and place AI

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:44.220
into a context-bounded box and
give it a well-defined task.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:46.620
And, similar to Ralph,
when you give him a task,

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:48.210
he's going to try
his hardest in order

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:50.160
to achieve what
you've asked him to.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:53.120
AI is also going to be able to
do that, but it is up to you

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:56.810
to basically go ahead and place
AI inside the right context.

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:58.190
This is called a Ralph loop.

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:03.380
So the way my team,
we use Fabric is --

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:06.210
let me go ahead and just
show the architecture --

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:09.360
is we have data that is
coming in, you know.

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.570
So, similar to
other enterprises,

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:14.910
we employ the Medallion
Architecture.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:16.470
So, on the top
left of my screen,

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:19.620
you can see we have data coming
in at very high velocity

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.750
from all sorts of data
sources, like, for example,

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.240
Piero was showing Kafka.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:27.210
A lot of data coming in,
being landed into Delta Lake.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:30.660
And then the idea is the data
that is inside of Delta Lake,

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:34.680
inside of a bronze format,
there's no schema.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:36.120
There is no structure
to the data.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:37.710
There's no columnar compression.

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:39.450
And the first thing
that we have to do

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:42.030
as a data engineer is
essentially apply schema

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:42.540
to the data.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:44.550
This is called schema on read.

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.680
And the schema might
be very complex.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:47.580
It might be XML.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:48.780
There might be nested JSON.

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:52.770
There could be all sorts of
different things that we have

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:56.670
to do in order to apply a
robust schema to the data,

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:59.760
get it into columnar format, and
store it inside of Delta Lake

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:03.600
in a well-compressed structure
so that, when you run queries

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.970
on the data, your queries
are effectively fast.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:10.500
So we're going to look at a
demonstration of this workflow

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:11.160
that I just mentioned.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:15.750
But the idea is, now that the
data has landed in OneLake,

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:18.990
what I want to do, actually, is,
because I'm working in VS Code,

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:24.750
I want to use the compute power
of my local machine in order

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:27.780
