00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:21.570
and the same kind of tools.

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:23.430
And, when you do that,
what you're going

00:23:23.430 --> 00:23:26.760
to find is the things that you
would have to do manually,

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:29.340
Copilot is able to basically
be grounded in this kind

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:34.230
of straight-line format in order
to go ahead and do the kind

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:36.030
of data engineering that you
would, except, you know,

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:37.440
you don't have to sit
here with your keyboard;

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:39.090
you can just go ahead and
work with your business,

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:41.550
work with human beings,
gather more requirements,

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:43.920
understand exactly what the
schema of the data should be,

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:46.650
doing the things that are more
impactful as a data engineer

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:49.620
to your business versus, you
know, sitting here and trying

00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:51.180
to basically
understand, you know,

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:53.640
what kind of gzip compression
and whatnot to apply, right?

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:55.410
We want to basically
delegate as much of that

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:57.270
to machines as possible.

00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:59.700
So, anyways, in the interest of
time, obviously, this is going

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:02.340
to be an end-to-end bronze
to silver pipeline.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:03.870
Let's keep Copilot running.

00:24:03.870 --> 00:24:05.580
But let me just walk you through
what the end-to-end is going

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:06.330
to look like.

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:09.930
So, again, you know Copilot is
going to go ahead and figure

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:11.670
out exactly what the
shape of this data is.

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:14.220
You can see it's already
scanning my code base.

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:15.750
So the next thing
is, as I mentioned,

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:17.520
the most important
thing that you can do

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:22.140
in this AI-native landscape is
the way you author your code has

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:24.330
to be done in an
AI-friendly manner.

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:27.510
So what I mean by that is
imagine you have an engineer

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:29.160
on your team who is
trying to figure

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:31.560
out how you write all
your ETL code, right?

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:34.320
If you don't have any sort
of structure to your code,

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:36.930
a human being is going to
struggle to read and understand

00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:38.520
and grok the code base.

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:39.600
Same thing with AI, right?

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:40.500
It has a context window.

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:41.880
You can't just read
a billion lines

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:44.430
of code and make sense of it.

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:47.010
So, in this case, you can see
that our team, we go ahead

00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:48.510
and interface all of our code,

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:51.240
just regular engineering
best practices,

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:54.540
with this extends data
transformer interface.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:57.780
So, for example, let's
say I have this data

00:24:57.780 --> 00:24:59.040
transformation code.

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:02.040
And I go ahead and say
extends data transformer.

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:04.980
You will notice that, even
though we have thousands

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:07.560
and thousands of lines of
code, every single one

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:11.310
of our data transformers
are using this data

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:12.270
transformer trait.

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:14.880
So, for example, if I
go ahead and search

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:18.900
for here extends data
transformer, you will notice

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:25.230
that any Spark code that I have
in my very large code base,

00:25:25.230 --> 00:25:27.060
all of them have to
basically go ahead

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:30.210
and implement this data
transformer interface.

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:33.330
And, if I go into this data
transformer, you will notice

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:36.240
that it just defines a
transformation method that takes

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:38.250
in a data frame and
spits out a data frame.

00:25:38.250 --> 00:25:39.360
Why is this important?

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:42.690
The reason it's important is
because the first thing we want

00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:46.140
to do with AI is we don't want
it to just write all sorts

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:48.780
of code that doesn't align
with our expectations,

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:50.220
code that is not testable.

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:53.070
We want to basically take
AI and bound it inside

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:54.990
of a type box that we define.

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:57.420
And the way I'm doing
that is every single one

00:25:57.420 --> 00:26:00.990
of my code classes are using
this data transformer.

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:02.940
And I basically
just told AI, hey.

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:04.230
Go ahead and search for it.

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:05.730
Ctrl F for it.

00:26:05.730 --> 00:26:09.930
And then go ahead and look at
my existing coding patterns,

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:13.710
and any new code that you're
going to generate has to comply

00:26:13.710 --> 00:26:15.840
with the code that I
have in my code base.

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:18.810
And so it's as if you're
onboarding a human engineer

00:26:18.810 --> 00:26:21.630
into your code base and you're
giving them the best practices

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:24.810
on how to code to align with
your expectations, right?

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:26.190
So you can see, for example,

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:28.830
like our friend here has
gone ahead and figured

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:31.620
out exactly what the
body format was.

00:26:31.620 --> 00:26:32.850
It's figured out the data type.

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:35.550
It's figured out
timestamp, JSON.

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:37.410
Essentially, everything
that you would have to do

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:39.450
as a human being, it's
going ahead and doing

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:44.130
that in a very deterministic
manner at machine speed, right.

00:26:44.130 --> 00:26:46.830
Again, you can go ahead
and tell AI, okay.

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:50.100
Try to reuse the code that
exists in my existing code base.

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:51.900
Don't just create a new class.

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:53.850
Try to put the code
in the best class

00:26:53.850 --> 00:26:56.190
where you think this
data belongs, right?

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:58.860
So you can see AI is going
and figuring out, oh.

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:02.460
You know, this code, I can go
ahead and reuse this T and I,

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:03.450
which is the name of my team,

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:05.370
JSON transformations
and so on, right.

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:08.940
So, anyway, so, at this point,
you can go ahead and AI is going

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:11.160
to present a comprehensive
design proposal

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:12.330
on what the schema
is going to be,

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:14.520
what the destination
table is going to be.

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:17.010
It's going to ask me and prompt,
you know, as I said, pause.

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:17.820
Do not proceed.

00:27:17.820 --> 00:27:21.840
And 10 times out of 10
almost AI is going to comply

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:23.310
with these instructions.

00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:26.670
And then you go ahead and
tell it to run the code

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:30.630
and actually write the code
after you've done validating it.

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:32.880
And then I'm just
giving it the kind

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:35.070
of coding standards
that our team uses.

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:37.950
Go ahead and register those new
tables that you're creating

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:41.010
for liquid clustering and
vacuuming and things like that.

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:43.920
And then, finally,
we tell AI, hey.

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:47.310
You went ahead and studied this
sample data, wrote this code.

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:48.990
Go ahead and
generate unit tests.

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:51.570
So, for example, you can see
it's basically prompting me

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:54.090
for exactly what
I want it to do.

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:56.970
But, when it gets to step
seven, it'll go ahead

00:27:56.970 --> 00:27:58.080
and generate unit tests.

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:01.020
So let's say we go ahead
and take the skill

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:03.480
that I was just showing you.

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:05.700
So let me just go ahead
and quickly show that.

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:08.520
So we have this open telemetry
columnar integration test.

00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:12.030
I have given AI
