should look like.

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:21.210
It knows exactly what my team
is expecting with regards

00:28:21.210 --> 00:28:23.370
to robust best practices.

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:25.890
And, once that's done,
it is going to go ahead

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:31.770
and generate the test code
that matches that standard.

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:34.380
And then we're actually
going to go ahead and ask it

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:37.320
to run the Spark job that it
just created using a Spark job

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:38.820
definition locally, right?

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:40.890
So, again, you need to have
a CLI tool that does that.

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:41.730
You take the driver class.

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:42.660
You run it.

00:28:42.660 --> 00:28:45.510
AI is basically going to
keep on running the code.

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:48.030
If it fails for some reason,
it's going to make code changes.

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:49.170
It's going to build the jar.

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:50.820
It's going to run the test.

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:52.650
It's going to effectively
do all of this

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:53.760
